# The Myth of the Echo Valley

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a misty valley. The mist distorted all sounds that crossed between them.

One village had abundant grain but little water. The other had a endless spring but rocky soil. To survive, they needed each other.

Each harvest, the Grain Village would send baskets across the valley on a rope bridge. But the mist was magical—whatever crossed through it multiplied threefold. One basket became three baskets on the other side.

The Water Village, blessed by this abundance, would send back what they deemed fair. But the return journey through the mist changed nothing—the amounts stayed the same.

